kground is the dominant color.\n 102:     - There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular\n 103:       one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it.\n 104:     - The frame interior is initially background.\n 105: \n 106:     Rule:\n 107:     - Clear the original sprite from its outside location.\n 108:     - Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to\n 109:       interior extremes.\n 110:     - Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of\n 111:       adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside\n 112:       those drawn segments.\n 113: \n 114:     Key insight:\n 115:     The outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior,\n 116:     not a bitmap to scale by block replication.\n 117:     \"\"\"\n 118:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n 119:     bg = get_bg(g)\n 120:     colors = colors_present(g, exclude={bg})\n 121:     assert len(colors) == 2, \"expected one frame color and one sprite color\"\n 122: \n 123:     frame_candidates: list[tuple[int, tuple[int, int, int, int]]] = []\n 124:     for color in colors:\n 125:         frame_bbox = _rect_frame_bbox(g == int(color))\n 126:         if frame_bbox is not None:\n 127:             frame_candidates.append((int(color), frame_bbox))\n 128:     assert len(frame_candidates) == 1, \"expected a unique rectangular frame\"\n 129: \n 130:     frame_color, (fr0, fc0, fr1, fc1) = frame_candidates[0]\n 131:     sprite_color = next(color for color in colors if color != frame_color)\n 132:     sprite_components = find_components(g, bg=bg, colors=[sprite_color], connectivity=4)\n 133:     assert len(sprite_components) == 1, \"expected a single 4-connected sprite\"\n 134:     frame_region = np.zeros_like(g, dtype=bool)\n 135:     frame_region[fr0 : fr1 + 1, fc0 : fc1 + 1] = True\n 136:     assert not np.any((g == sprite_color) & frame_region), \"sprite must start